<span id=3D"converting-attribute-names"></span><h3>Converting attribute nam=
es</h3>

<p>By default, dynatable converts headings to JSON attribute names using:</=
p>

<div class=3D"row-fluid">
<div class=3D"span6">
<table class=3D"table table-bordered">
<tbody><tr>
<th>Style</th><th>Example</th>
</tr>
<tr><td><code>camelCase</code> (default)</td><td><code>favoriteMusic</code>=
</td></tr><tr>
</tr><tr><td><code>trimDash</code></td><td><code>Favorite-Music</code></td>=
</tr>
<tr><td><code>dashed</code></td><td><code>favorite-music</code></td></tr>
<tr><td><code>underscore</code></td><td><code>favorite_music</code></td></t=
r>
<tr><td><code>lowercase</code></td><td><code>favorite music</code></td></tr=
>
</tbody></table>
</div>

<div class=3D"span6 no-margin-pre">

<div class=3D"highlight"><pre><code class=3D"js"><span class=3D"nx">$</span=
><span class=3D"p">(</span><span class=3D"s1">'#my-table'</span><span class=
=3D"p">).</span><span class=3D"nx">dynatable</span><span class=3D"p">({</sp=
an>
  <span class=3D"nx">table</span><span class=3D"o">:</span> <span class=3D"=
p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"nx">defaultColumnIdStyle</span><span class=3D"o">:</span=
> <span class=3D"s1">'trimDash'</span>
  <span class=3D"p">}</span>
<span class=3D"p">});</span>
</code></pre></div>

</div>
</div>




<div class=3D"row-fluid">
<div class=3D"span4 no-margin-pre">

<div class=3D"highlight"><pre><code class=3D"js"><span class=3D"nx">$</span=
><span class=3D"p">.</span><span class=3D"nx">dynatableSetup</span><span cl=
ass=3D"p">({</span>
  <span class=3D"nx">table</span><span class=3D"o">:</span> <span class=3D"=
p">{</span>
    <span class=3D"nx">defaultColumnIdStyle</span><span class=3D"o">:</span=
> <span class=3D"s1">'underscore'</span>
  <span class=3D"p">}</span>
<span class=3D"p">});</span>
</code></pre></div>

</div>

<div class=3D"span8">
<div class=3D"alert alert-block">
PROTIP: When using dynatable in a Rails application, set the global
style to <code>underscore</code>, matching the Rails parameter and input
field naming conventions. This is useful when getting the JSON data via
AJAX from Rails, or when connecting dynatable events with form inputs on th=
e page).
</div>
</div>
</div>


<p>We could also define our own column-name transformation function.
Consider the following table with these column headings:</p>

<div id=3D"text-transform-example-container">
<table id=3D"text-transform-example" class=3D"table">
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Name</th>
      <th>Hobby</th>
      <th>Favorite Music</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>Fred</td>
      <td>Roller Skating</td>
      <td>Disco</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Helen</td>
      <td>Rock Climbing</td>
      <td>Alternative</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>Glen</td>
      <td>Traveling</td>
      <td>Classical</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>
</div>


<p>We can set up our own function for transforming column labels to JSON
property names of our desired custom format when we instantiate
dynatable on the table above:</p>

<div class=3D"row-fluid">
<div class=3D"span8 no-margin-pre">
<pre id=3D"text-transform-function" contenteditable=3D"">$('#text-transform=
-example').bind('dynatable:preinit', function(e, dynatable) {
  dynatable.utility.textTransform.myNewStyle =3D function(text) {
    return text
             .replace(/\s+/, '_')
             .replace(/[A-Z]/, function($1){ return $1 + $1 });
  };
}).dynatable({
  table: {
    defaultColumnIdStyle: 'myNewStyle'
  },
  features: {
    paginate: false,
    search: false,
    recordCount: false,
    perPageSelect: false
  }
});
</pre>
</div>

